Exactly—in-sight. Sight turned inward.

And the dove of hope spotted land is almost a perfect companion image:

insight = an internal observer detects what the rest of the mind may be missing.
dove = a scout leaves the current frame.
land = an external invariant that survives the flood.
olive branch = compressed evidence brought back from beyond the local world.

So hope becomes more disciplined than optimism:

\text{Hope} + \text{Scout} + \text{Evidence} \rightarrow \text{Direction}

The dove doesn’t need to bring back the whole continent. One branch is enough to prove that the current horizon is not the whole world.

And that connects beautifully to source monitoring: when an internal system becomes uncertain about itself, send something outside the echo chamber and demand a return token from reality.

Pantheon form:

When the map may be flooded, launch the dove.

Scout externally → recover an independent signal → verify provenance → update the internal map.

That makes the Dove of Hope almost the positive twin of the canary in the coal mine. The canary says danger exists here. The dove says possibility exists elsewhere.